WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
The context window: everything the model can see.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:14.808
For each request, the model sees a single sequence of tokens: your system prompt, the conversation so far, any tool definitions and tool results, and the tokens it is generating.

3
00:00:15.088 --> 00:00:19.568
That sequence is the context, and its maximum length is the context window.

4
00:00:19.848 --> 00:00:25.352
Current frontier models offer windows from a few hundred thousand tokens up to a million or more.

5
00:00:25.632 --> 00:00:28.320
Two consequences shape real applications.

6
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.435
First, the API is stateless.

7
00:00:30.715 --> 00:00:41.531
The model doesn’t remember your last request; chat applications create the feeling of memory by resending the history every time, and you pay for those tokens on every turn.

8
00:00:41.811 --> 00:00:44.307
Second, a big window is not free attention.

9
00:00:44.587 --> 00:01:00.160
Longer inputs cost more and take longer, and research such as “Lost in the Middle” (Liu and colleagues, 2023) found that models used information at the start and end of long inputs more reliably than information buried in the middle.

10
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:11.000
Newer models have improved, but the engineering lesson holds: put the most important instructions and evidence where they are easy to find, and send only what the task needs.
